00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:55,460
Look, Carol, will you just tell me, do I stink?

254
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:59,800
Uh, yes. As an actor, as an actor, Carol.

255
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:01,800
Come on, I mean, I don't even know if I'm good anymore.

256
00:13:01,840 --> 00:13:03,080
I don't even know what's good.

257
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,520
I mean, how does anybody know?

258
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:05,680
You just know.

259
00:13:05,840 --> 00:13:08,280
For instance, when it comes to intellect, well?

260
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:12,340
So how do you know you're well?

261
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:16,300
Very simple. I got straight As.

262
00:13:16,300 --> 00:13:17,120
Oh, come on, Carol.

263
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:18,560
What the heck do those grades mean now?

264
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:22,280
Well... Come on, Carol, they mean absolutely diddly in the real world.

265
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:23,040
You know that.

266
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:26,720
Well, I'm head of punctuation at a prominent publishing house.

267
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:29,460
Ooh, saving a lot of lives there, aren't you?

268
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:33,080
Come on, forget about the stupid grades and the pointless job.

269
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:36,080
I mean, do you have any real proof that you're good?

270
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:42,800
No. Oh. Okay, you're worthless.

271
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:43,980
Let's get back to my problems.

272
00:13:45,080 --> 00:13:46,720
I need another piece of cake.

273
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:47,680
Oh, Carol, come on.

274
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:48,520
I am in pain here.

275
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:50,180
Would you forget about your useless life?

276
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:54,140
Oh. I can't wait.

277
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:55,580
Come on, Carol, just help me.

278
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:56,880
I can't help anybody.

279
00:13:57,040 --> 00:13:59,560
I'm useless. Carol, don't eat in your room.

280
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:01,980
Don't worry. It'll be gone before I get there.

281
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:04,680
Mike, what's going on?

282
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:07,780
Oh, nothing. I just asked, Carol, how do you know if you're good?

283
00:14:08,820 --> 00:14:09,820
What's this about, Mike?

284
00:14:10,040 --> 00:14:13,960
Well, okay. See, it's just that seeing everyone go ape -chiping

285
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:16,220
Oh, she was wonderful.

286
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:18,440
Now, that is what acting is all about.

287
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:20,400
See, that's what scares me.

288
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:22,020
I mean, I thought she stunk.

289
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:24,380
Apparently, I don't have a clue as to what acting's all about.

290
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:28,320
Come on, guys.

291
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:29,820
I mean, how do I know if I'm good?

292
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,780
Well, what you're wrestling with here is an essential question that everybody asks.

293
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:35,980
Everybody wants to know if I'm good?

294
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:38,620
No, Mike, if they're good.

295
00:14:39,380 --> 00:14:41,360
Well, Dad, how does a person know if they're good?

296
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:44,600
I mean, Mom, okay, how do you know if you're a good journalist.

297
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:51,240
Well, Mike, I've been around, and I know how to put together a story, and my resume

298
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:52,360
is quite impressive.

299
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:56,720
Come on, Mom, you've always complained about how you worked at a dinky paper on a

300
00:14:56,720 --> 00:14:57,920
bozo TV station.

301
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,060
Who hasn't won any awards?

302
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:01,880
Sure I have.

303
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:04,140
I won the Working Mother of the Year Award.

304
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:05,920
What did you call it?

305
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:08,680
The Monkey Award.

306
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:12,320
The Monkey Award they had to give to somebody.

307
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:15,200
Thanks, Mike. Mike, come on.

308
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:17,640
Awards aren't the only way that you know you're good.

309
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,820
It's what your mom does that counts.

310
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:24,380
Oh. Oh, so you're saying since you've been writing on your own, you've sold a lot of

311
00:15:24,380 --> 00:15:28,780
articles? One. One?

312
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:32,440
But I have had many encouraging no -thank -you letters.

313
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,020
Some of the finest publications in the country don't want to work with me.

314
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:40,840
So you guys are guessing just like I am.

315
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:44,080
Mike, your mother is good at what she does.

316
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:46,300
Dad, I'm not asking if she's good at what she does.

317
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:48,000
I'm asking how do you know?

318
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:50,580
Yeah, Jason. How?

319
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:53,480
I'll tell you.

320
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:54,620
I can tell you.

321
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:55,680
It's, uh, as...

322
00:15:55,680 --> 00:15:57,680
Well, in journalism, for instance, you have to...

323
00:15:57,680 --> 00:15:58,640
It's... You look...

324
00:15:58,640 --> 00:15:59,480
It's like an onion.

325
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:00,540
It's an onion.

326
00:16:00,640 --> 00:16:05,860
And if you peel the layers of journalism, it's...

327
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:07,660
Oh, well, let me use psychiatry.

328
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:09,620
In my profession, I can illustrate with that.

329
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:15,020
In my case, if I have helped a patient, I feel terrific.

330
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:18,540
If I help a patient and they come to me and they say, I don't need to see anymore,

331
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:23,640
I'm fine. Dad, people who are a little off tell you you're good and you believe

332
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:27,620
them? Mike. Dad, think.

333
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:32,300
I mean, what if these people who tell you that they're cured are just cutting you

334
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:33,900
loose so they can find a good psychiatrist?

335
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:39,600
I am a good psychiatrist.

336
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:41,160
Dad, come on.

337
00:16:41,180 --> 00:16:44,660
I mean, maybe the people who keep coming back, you're not helping.

338
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:48,940
And the ones who tell you they're cured are really trying to find somebody who can.

339
00:16:49,100 --> 00:16:51,800
Just because I lose two patients to Dr.

340
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:53,480
Harry the Hot Tub Strickland...

341
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:55,580
I didn't mean to upset you.

342
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:56,940
Okay, all right.

343
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:00,600
Well, I can tell you this, Mike, that only somebody who can ask himself that

344
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:02,940
question has any chance of ever answering it.

345
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:08,140
Yeah. I just wish there was a way for a person to tell if he's really good.

346
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:12,750
If you find it, let me know.

347
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:22,020
I don't belong here.

348
00:17:25,380 --> 00:17:26,720
None of this are you, Don't.

349
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,840
Professor Thorne, what are you doing here?

350
00:17:30,100 --> 00:17:30,920
I have tenure.

351
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:32,120
What's your excuse?

352
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:38,600
I'm trespassing. Can I ask you something?

353
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:43,400
What? How do you know if you're good?

354
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:46,360
How do I know I'm good?

355
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:47,600
How do you know you're good?

356
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:49,200
Or how does one know one's good?

357
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:51,960
Uh, the last thing you said.

358
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:54,840
Ah. One of life's essential questions.

359
00:17:55,300 --> 00:17:57,000
Oh, you sound just like my dad.

360
00:17:57,380 --> 00:17:58,420
Your father's British?

361
00:17:58,860 --> 00:18:01,080
No. No, no, no.

362
00:18:01,340 --> 00:18:04,260
Just acting's weird, you know?

363
00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:10,380
I mean, it's not like being a tightrope walker, Where you know you're good if you

364
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:13,320
don't die. You can die in the theater, believe me.

365
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:15,300
Come on, Professor, I'm being serious.

366
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:17,740
I mean, if you're an actor,

367
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:21,640
how do you know that you even have the right to be on the stage?

368
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:25,400
Are you telling me, Mr.

369
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:27,980
Siever, that you have doubts about your abilities?

370
00:18:29,020 --> 00:18:32,480
You always struck me as being impervious to introspection.

371
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:37,660
As what? As a confident dude.

372
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:41,620
Oh, well, I was.

373
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:43,000
Up till tonight.

374
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:48,600
Mr. Siever, as I told you, your scene was the best of the evening.

375
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:52,360
It showed you had an excellent grasp on what good theater is.

376
00:18:52,720 --> 00:18:54,800
Well, see, that's the problem, because I thought it stunk.

377
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:56,680
You did? Completely.

378
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:00,920
And here I am jealous of you.

379
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:06,300
Of what you've accomplished at your age, of what the future holds for you, and you

380
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:07,160
thought the scene stunk.

381
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:08,400
Wait a minute.

382
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:09,720
How could you be jealous of me?

383
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:10,360
You're the teacher.

384
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:12,580
But 30 years ago, I was you.

385
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:14,360
Or at least I presumed I was.

386
00:19:15,380 --> 00:19:18,140
A young, talented buck ready to lick the world.

387
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:20,820
I didn't. I'm here.

388
00:19:22,380 --> 00:19:24,120
So you're saying you don't like teaching drama?

389
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:24,780
No, no, no.

390
00:19:24,780 --> 00:19:25,380
Don't get me wrong.

391
00:19:25,380 --> 00:19:26,480
I love it.

392
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:29,940
And how many people can make a living by doing what they really love?

393
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:33,160
But if I had another chance, I'd...

394
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:37,940
The point is, you have that chance.

395
00:19:39,580 --> 00:19:41,320
Yeah, but how do I know if I'm good?

396
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:45,560
Mike, the answer is not nearly so mystical as you imagine.

397
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:49,500
It's not? Listen to what people have to tell you.

398
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:53,980
But the trick is to be selective in the people you choose to listen to.

399
00:19:54,540 --> 00:19:58,040
I mean, choose people you respect, people of taste.

400
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:00,440
You mean like critics?

401
00:20:00,960 --> 00:20:02,400
I'm being serious, Mr.

402
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:06,900
C. Well, then, how do I know who to listen to?

403
00:20:08,700 --> 00:20:13,600
Years ago, I had a small part in a Joseph Papp production of Twelfth Night.

404
00:20:14,760 --> 00:20:18,580
Afterwards, my dear sweet mother gushed all over me, told me I was wonderful.

405
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:19,620
And you believed it?

406
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:22,300
Certainly not. Daffy old bat.

407
00:20:23,880 --> 00:20:25,160
But I digress.

408
00:20:25,700 --> 00:20:28,080
Anyway, Mr. Papp himself

409
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:30,420
spoke to me after a matter day.

410
00:20:30,460 --> 00:20:32,640
Now, he didn't have to speak to me, but he chose to.

411
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:38,000
He said, young man, you have promise.

412
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:39,880
Now that, Mr.

413
00:20:39,980 --> 00:20:41,480
Siever, I believed.

414
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:46,700
Professor Thorne. Yes?

415
00:20:48,900 --> 00:20:51,340
Am I any good at this?

416
00:20:53,840 --> 00:20:56,200
Well, Mr. Siever, I'll say this.

417
00:20:57,400 --> 00:20:58,340
You have promise.

418
00:21:01,740 --> 00:21:08,500
Yeah? Yeah. I do, don't I?

419
00:21:08,960 --> 00:21:10,560
I believe I just said that.

420
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:17,400
Right. Thanks. Mr.

421
00:21:17,480 --> 00:21:22,800
Seiver, do you think I'm any good as a teacher?

422
00:21:24,920 --> 00:21:25,840
You're asking me?

423
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:32,000
Yes. Well, I'll say this.

424
00:21:33,300 --> 00:21:34,140
You have a promise.

425
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:37,920
Thank you, Mr.

426
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:41,100
Siever. No sweat.

427
00:21:50,840 --> 00:21:52,980
All the world's a stage.

428
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:55,980
And all the men and women merely players.

429
00:21:57,460 --> 00:21:59,860
They have their exits and their entrances,

430
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:04,020
and one man in his time plays many parts.

431
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:19,420
Applause We'll be back in a moment.

432
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:31,480
MUSIC
